Subject:      Raise MAX_REPETITION_THRESHOLD from 2000 to 100000 for complex tool schemas
Applies to:   TheTom/llama-cpp-turboquant @ 337f08e82a861194fd5fc93121205c7c6bc81ddf
              (src/llama-grammar.cpp)

WHAT IT FIXES
=============
`MAX_REPETITION_THRESHOLD` is a compile-time cap on grammar repetition expansion,
and both gates that read it THROW:

  * :494  `n_prev_rules * total_rules >= MAX_REPETITION_THRESHOLD`
          → "number of rules that are going to be repeated multiplied by the new
          repetition exceeds sane defaults, please reduce the number of
          repetitions or rule complexity"
  * :652  `min_times > MAX_REPETITION_THRESHOLD || (has_max && max_times > ...)`
          → "number of repetitions exceeds sane defaults, please reduce the number
          of repetitions"

At 2000 that rejects the GBNF `json-schema-to-grammar` emits for a realistic
OpenAI-style tool list: one 47-parameter tool with nested objects already expands
to ~500 rules, and the first gate compares with `>=`, so a schema that merely
REACHES the cap is refused too.

⇒ THE FAILURE IS AT REQUEST TIME, NOT AT STARTUP, which is exactly what makes it
worth an anchor guard. The image builds, llama-server starts, `/health` returns 200
and droste's healthcheck is green — and every tool-calling request comes back
"failed to parse grammar". There is no symptom anywhere a build or a probe looks.

Raising the constant costs nothing at runtime: both gates run only while PARSING a
grammar, never during inference, and 100000 is still finite, so runaway expansion
is still refused.

WHY WE CARRY IT
===============
🚨 THIS PATCH IS PERMANENT, NOT A STOPGAP, and its provenance is easy to misread.
The issue above is against kyuz0's amd-strix-halo-toolboxes — a third-party image
project in the same niche as ours — not against llama.cpp. Its `completed`
resolution is kyuz0 rebuilding his own images with the same raise ("Thank you,
rebuilding now"). No change was ever proposed to, or made in, llama.cpp.

Verified 2026-09-01: ggml-org/llama.cpp master still says
`#define MAX_REPETITION_THRESHOLD 2000`, and so does TheTom/llama-cpp-turboquant's
own default branch. Upstream PRs 21003 and 21139, which would make the constant
configurable at runtime and retire the need for any patch, are both OPEN and
unmerged.

We are not filing and not chasing it (standing project ruling: "Someone else might
refile, but not us."). NOBODY IS WATCHING FOR THIS TO BECOME UNNECESSARY. Treat it
as load-bearing indefinitely and expect it to survive every pin bump.

WHAT WOULD MAKE IT REMOVABLE (a condition to NOTICE, not an outcome to expect)
=============================================================================
If a future pin raises the constant itself, removes it, renames it, or makes it
configurable, this patch stops applying cleanly and the anchor guard in
scaffolding/Container.llama-build goes red. THAT GUARD IS THE ONLY THING THAT WILL
EVER TELL US. When it fires, read the new source before assuming anything: it may
mean upstream fixed it (delete this file and the guard), or merely that the fork
moved and the hunk needs re-deriving at the new SHA.

⚠️ AND IT CAN STOP MATTERING WITHOUT FAILING TO APPLY. If the macro survives but
nothing reads it any more, this patch applies perfectly and changes nothing. That
is why the guard also asserts the macro is still READ, not merely still defined.

THE BLANK CONTEXT LINE IS A SINGLE SPACE — DO NOT LET AN EDITOR STRIP IT
=======================================================================
The hunk's third context line is the empty line above the `#define`, and a
canonical unified diff spells it as one space. Tools that trim trailing whitespace
turn it into a truly empty line, which makes GNU patch 2.8 apply this with fuzz 1
— and the guard runs `patch -F0`, so it would then fail outright. That is the
strictness working as intended; the fix is to restore the space, never to relax
the fuzz.

